# Every path that is not a real file becomes the app, so deep links work.
#
# It is tempting to make /assets/* answer 404 instead, because a hashed file
# that has not propagated yet — the ordinary state of things for a second or
# two mid-deploy — currently comes back as index.html with a 200, and a browser
# handed a page where it asked for a module refuses it.
#
# Do not. It was tried: Cloudflare Pages needs a real destination for a 404
# status, and the moment a 404.html exists in the output Pages starts serving it
# for every unmatched path, ahead of this rule. Deep links to /meals/trends and
# the rest began 404ing, which is a worse failure than the one being fixed.
#
# The real defence lives in public/sw.js, which refuses to cache or serve that
# fallback under an asset's URL, and in public/boot-guard.js, which recovers a
# load where nothing mounted.
/*    /index.html   200
